WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.733 --> 00:00:01.934
-My first guest

00:00:01.934 --> 00:00:03.403
is a four-time
Golden Globe-nominated actress

00:00:03.403 --> 00:00:05.939
and a "New York Times"
best-selling author.

00:00:05.939 --> 00:00:08.474
She's also the co-founder
of a new wine brand,

00:00:08.474 --> 00:00:11.143
called Avaline,
which is available now.

00:00:11.143 --> 00:00:15.147
Here is my pal, Cameron Diaz!
Hey!

00:00:15.147 --> 00:00:17.883
Aww.
I miss your face.

00:00:17.883 --> 00:00:19.718
You look great, buddy.

00:00:19.718 --> 00:00:21.286
-Thanks.
So good to see you.

00:00:21.286 --> 00:00:22.489
I can't believe this.

00:00:22.489 --> 00:00:24.557
I was hoping to come
see y'all this summer.

00:00:24.557 --> 00:00:26.325
-I know.
'Cause I haven't --

00:00:26.325 --> 00:00:29.462
We haven't seen you
since you had the baby.

00:00:29.462 --> 00:00:32.331
-Nope.
-How is it being quarantined

00:00:32.331 --> 00:00:34.900
with the baby?

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:37.236
-Heaven.
It's been heaven, Jimmy.

00:00:37.236 --> 00:00:40.840
You know. You just got to spend
four months with the girls,

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:44.744
and you get to have every
moment, and it goes so fast.

00:00:44.744 --> 00:00:47.780
And you really don't understand
that when people

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:51.451
have always said to me, you
know, "Really enjoy the time

00:00:51.451 --> 00:00:53.419
when they're babies.
It's the best."

00:00:53.419 --> 00:00:57.056
And literally, every single day,
there's just --

00:00:57.056 --> 00:00:59.425
there's leaps and bounds,
and these things that happen

00:00:59.425 --> 00:01:02.295
that she's not the same baby
she was yesterday.

00:01:02.295 --> 00:01:04.463
-Yeah, I know. People always
said that to me, too,

00:01:04.463 --> 00:01:06.632
and I was like, "All right,
all right, all right."

00:01:06.632 --> 00:01:07.733
They're like,
"No, it goes fast."

00:01:07.733 --> 00:01:09.935
I go, "I understand.
I heard you."

00:01:09.935 --> 00:01:12.805
And, gosh, they're right.

00:01:12.805 --> 00:01:14.707
-Where was -- What --
Where was yesterday?

00:01:14.707 --> 00:01:19.011
Yesterday is literally gone,
and today is a new day,

00:01:19.011 --> 00:01:20.279
and tomorrow will be a new day

00:01:20.279 --> 00:01:21.681
that she's a completely
different baby.

00:01:21.681 --> 00:01:25.651
But it's so gratifying to
actually get to see that growth

00:01:25.651 --> 00:01:29.155
and to be a part of it
and to help

00:01:29.155 --> 00:01:33.192
just let her be her,
and it's just amazing.

00:01:33.192 --> 00:01:34.894
I just --
It's the best thing

00:01:34.894 --> 00:01:37.163
that ever happened
to both Benj and I.

00:01:37.163 --> 00:01:39.932
We're so --
We're just so happy.

00:01:39.932 --> 00:01:42.434
-We were so happy
when you called us.

00:01:42.434 --> 00:01:45.604
We were just crying
and jumping up and down.

00:01:45.604 --> 00:01:49.709
Has your photos on your phone
completely changed?

00:01:49.709 --> 00:01:51.010
-[ Laughing ]
I mean...

00:01:51.010 --> 00:01:54.380
Well, first off,
the sad thing is that my phone

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:57.784
used to be pictures of my dogs
and my dogs' poop.

00:01:57.784 --> 00:02:00.753
Because I have four of them.

00:02:00.753 --> 00:02:03.656
You know that you're just
constantly sending pictures

00:02:03.656 --> 00:02:07.126
of your dog poop
to your veterinarian, going,

00:02:07.126 --> 00:02:09.295
"Is this normal?
Is this okay?"

00:02:09.295 --> 00:02:13.900
Now, thankfully, I can say
that my pictures are no longer

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:16.268
of my dog poop,
and I'm also gonna say

00:02:16.268 --> 00:02:17.704
they're not of my child's poop.

00:02:17.704 --> 00:02:19.472
-Oh, that was my next
follow-up question!

00:02:19.472 --> 00:02:20.673
[ Both laugh ]

00:02:20.673 --> 00:02:23.576
The pediatrician's
getting pictures now.

00:02:23.576 --> 00:02:25.978
-We're very confident in being
able to decipher her poop,

00:02:25.978 --> 00:02:29.415
'cause it's very similar to...

00:02:29.415 --> 00:02:33.052
-Oh, are you --
are you doing the whole --

00:02:33.052 --> 00:02:36.922
all the movies and the shows
and "Baby Shark."

00:02:36.922 --> 00:02:38.991
Is that happening in your house?
-Oh, my God.

00:02:38.991 --> 00:02:41.260
-Let me just tell you,
it's literally,

00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:45.732
we have "Baby Shark" on
rotation, we have Elmo's song,

00:02:45.732 --> 00:02:48.401
we have "Sesame street."
-Yes.

00:02:48.401 --> 00:02:50.970
-It's crazy. We're just
jamming out over there.

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:52.805
-Get ready for that --
-The thing that

00:02:52.805 --> 00:02:55.207
I don't understand, like,
you go, "I will never

00:02:55.207 --> 00:02:57.844
play that music for my child.
That's crazy."

00:02:57.844 --> 00:03:02.848
But you watch their response,
and they're so taken by it.

00:03:02.848 --> 00:03:05.718
It's like she changes.
She's like -- [ Gasps ]

00:03:05.718 --> 00:03:07.820
"My people!"
[ Both laugh ]

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:10.156
-Isn't it the greatest thing?
Honestly, we --

00:03:10.156 --> 00:03:11.824
My kids are little late
to the party,

00:03:11.824 --> 00:03:13.960
but they're getting into
Disney songs right now.

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:17.797
It is Disney princess,
Idina Menzel all day long.

00:03:17.797 --> 00:03:20.432
And when that song came out,
at first, you know,

00:03:20.432 --> 00:03:22.735
we were making jokes
and all this stuff.

00:03:22.735 --> 00:03:25.137
Can we stop listening
to the song, "Let It Go"?

00:03:25.137 --> 00:03:28.908
It's the best song in the world
to watch your kids sing the song

00:03:28.908 --> 00:03:32.244
and get into it
and close their eyes.

00:03:32.244 --> 00:03:33.913
It's my favorite --
I love it.

00:03:33.913 --> 00:03:35.447
Dude, I'm so the opposite
where I was.

00:03:35.447 --> 00:03:37.984
It's, like, all changing.

00:03:37.984 --> 00:03:41.621
-When we go on car drives,
Benj will put on Cuban --

00:03:41.621 --> 00:03:45.557
Afro-Cuban music,
and she is in her car seat,

00:03:45.557 --> 00:03:48.328
and her little legs
are, like, kicking.

00:03:48.328 --> 00:03:51.497
I'm like, "Yep, that's my girl."

00:03:51.497 --> 00:03:53.432
-Is he writing songs
for the baby?

00:03:53.432 --> 00:03:55.768
He must be.
-He's got about a dozen now.

00:03:55.768 --> 00:03:58.471
They're amazing.
That's what I sing all day

00:03:58.471 --> 00:04:02.308
in my head are his songs to her,
because she just loves it.

00:04:02.308 --> 00:04:04.776
She just is like, you know,

00:04:04.776 --> 00:04:07.714
looks at her daddy,
and she just loves him so much.

00:04:07.714 --> 00:04:09.481
She thinks he's the bee's knees.

00:04:09.481 --> 00:04:12.785
It -- oh -- It makes my heart
just want to explode.

00:04:12.785 --> 00:04:16.589
-I know Benj is involved
with this -- is it Veeps?

00:04:16.589 --> 00:04:18.057
-Yes, with Veeps.

00:04:18.057 --> 00:04:20.259
That's his platform
him and Joel started.

00:04:20.259 --> 00:04:23.228
Artists can't go out and tour,
and we all know that's how

00:04:23.228 --> 00:04:26.465
musicians and all artists,
people who pay --

00:04:26.465 --> 00:04:28.100
you know, get paid
to be in a room,

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:29.969
they can't do that any longer.

00:04:29.969 --> 00:04:32.137
So, they created
this platform where,

00:04:32.137 --> 00:04:35.641
if artists can, you know,

00:04:35.641 --> 00:04:39.111
take their livestreams
and make a living.

00:04:39.111 --> 00:04:42.314
Because touring is not
coming back anytime soon.

00:04:42.314 --> 00:04:46.552
-Do you remember
your first concert as a kid?

00:04:46.552 --> 00:04:49.421
-Yes, I do.
-Oh, wait a sec. All right.

00:04:49.421 --> 00:04:52.425
Wait. I'm already mad, 'cause
that's a very confident --

00:04:52.425 --> 00:04:53.959
Yeah. What was it?

00:04:53.959 --> 00:04:56.562
-Okay.
So, when I was 11,

00:04:56.562 --> 00:05:00.933
1984, Van Halen, "Jump,"
was, like, my life.

00:05:00.933 --> 00:05:02.401
It was my life.

00:05:02.401 --> 00:05:05.738
-No. You did not see
Van Halen in 1984.

00:05:05.738 --> 00:05:08.474
-At the Forum
here in Los Angeles.

00:05:08.474 --> 00:05:10.443
My mother came home from work.

00:05:10.443 --> 00:05:12.778
I had been sick that day.
She came home from work,

00:05:12.778 --> 00:05:15.181
she said,
"I got a surprise for you,

00:05:15.181 --> 00:05:17.016
but you have
to go to school tomorrow."

00:05:17.016 --> 00:05:18.417
And I was like,
"Okay. What is it?"

00:05:18.417 --> 00:05:20.719
And she was like --
One of her clients

00:05:20.719 --> 00:05:24.924
had given her, like,
the loge seats on the side of,

00:05:24.924 --> 00:05:31.030
like, right down by the stage
for Van Halen.

00:05:31.030 --> 00:05:32.398
They were like,
"We don't need these,"

00:05:32.398 --> 00:05:34.567
and my mom was like,
"My daughter loves Van Halen."

00:05:34.567 --> 00:05:37.070
So it's me, 11, my sister, 13.

00:05:37.070 --> 00:05:42.141
My mom goes, she drops us off
at the front where we go in.

00:05:42.141 --> 00:05:44.710
She's in her Volkswagen bus,
she goes and parks,

00:05:44.710 --> 00:05:48.047
she does her needlepoint
and drinks her wine

00:05:48.047 --> 00:05:51.417
and eats her cheese in the car
while we're in the Forum

00:05:51.417 --> 00:05:52.919
screaming our heads off.

00:05:52.919 --> 00:05:55.154
And, Jimmy, when I say
screaming our heads off,

00:05:55.154 --> 00:05:58.223
I mean, like,
I'm like, "I love you, Eddie!"

00:05:58.223 --> 00:06:01.360
Like -- Like, blood
going down my throat

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:04.296
'cause I'm screaming so loud,
like crying.

00:06:04.296 --> 00:06:06.031
It was, like, a Beatles moment.

00:06:06.031 --> 00:06:08.868
And there was, like,
these girls behind us who were,

00:06:08.868 --> 00:06:13.839
like, mature, young women
who, like, kept hitting us

00:06:13.839 --> 00:06:16.676
and going, "Quiet!
Stop screaming! Stop it!"

00:06:16.676 --> 00:06:18.344
The women in front of us
were turning around, going,

00:06:18.344 --> 00:06:22.014
"Be quiet!" and me and my sister
were there just, like, dying.

00:06:22.014 --> 00:06:25.952
We were losing our [bleep]

00:06:25.952 --> 00:06:28.553
-And they are
an amazing live band.

00:06:28.553 --> 00:06:31.324
I mean,
they are incredible live.

00:06:31.324 --> 00:06:32.558
-Okay.

00:06:32.558 --> 00:06:36.095
David Lee Roth,
at that time, in 1984,

00:06:36.095 --> 00:06:39.965
was still capable of jumping
across the entire --

00:06:39.965 --> 00:06:41.734
-With the boom box?
[ Both laugh ]

00:06:41.734 --> 00:06:44.737
-He was like Michael Jordan
before Michael Jordan.

00:06:44.737 --> 00:06:47.606
-[ Laughing ]

00:06:47.606 --> 00:06:50.977
-He could jump that far
for that long.

00:06:50.977 --> 00:06:54.380
-I got to see them when they had
a little reunion concert

00:06:54.380 --> 00:06:57.482
at Café Wha? in New York,
in a tiny club,

00:06:57.482 --> 00:07:02.421
and they sang "Panama,"
which is unbelievable, the song.

00:07:02.421 --> 00:07:03.956
And they got to that one point,
where, like...

00:07:03.956 --> 00:07:07.460
♪ And da, da,
ain't no stopping us ♪

00:07:07.460 --> 00:07:10.095
And we all screamed like
♪ Pana -- ♪

00:07:10.095 --> 00:07:11.631
But the band
didn't play anything.

00:07:11.631 --> 00:07:13.866
They were like --
[ Clears throat, sniffs ]

00:07:13.866 --> 00:07:16.102
-Shut up.
You know what?

00:07:16.102 --> 00:07:18.070
-And then, they looked back,
and they're like...

00:07:18.070 --> 00:07:20.473
♪ Pana-- ♪
And we all went, "Aah!"

00:07:20.473 --> 00:07:23.442
It was awesome!
It was awesome.

00:07:23.442 --> 00:07:25.311
-You're like, "Whaaat?"
-Yes!

00:07:25.311 --> 00:07:26.812
It was the greatest thing
I've ever seen.

00:07:26.812 --> 00:07:29.481
Best move in rock 'n' roll.
It so worked.

00:07:29.481 --> 00:07:31.450
It tricked all of us.

00:07:31.450 --> 00:07:35.054
-Took it to the next level.
-Oh, I...

00:07:35.054 --> 00:07:37.790
-You know what I did
for my sister's 40th birthday?

00:07:37.790 --> 00:07:40.526
Is Van Halen
was on their reunion tour

00:07:40.526 --> 00:07:43.095
with David Lee Roth.
-Yeah.

00:07:43.095 --> 00:07:48.834
So, I got tickets at, like,
the place that I could guess

00:07:48.834 --> 00:07:54.072
was the closest spot to where
we were when we were 11 and 13.

00:07:54.072 --> 00:07:57.343
And it was for the two of us,
and we went to The Forum,

00:07:57.343 --> 00:08:00.445
and we went and saw Van Halen.
-That's cool.

00:08:00.445 --> 00:08:02.047
That is cool.
See?

00:08:02.047 --> 00:08:05.050
You just took cool one,
you took the next step cooler.

00:08:05.050 --> 00:08:07.819
My first concert
was Weird Al Yankovic.

00:08:07.819 --> 00:08:10.289
Oh, by the way,
that makes total sense.

00:08:10.289 --> 00:08:11.990
[ Both laugh ]

00:08:11.990 --> 00:08:14.460
I loved it,
I loved it.

00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:16.228
You know, looking through
all your credits

00:08:16.228 --> 00:08:19.798
and all the films you've made,
I didn't know --

00:08:19.798 --> 00:08:21.266
I don't know why --
I just assumed

00:08:21.266 --> 00:08:22.534
you already had been in movies,

00:08:22.534 --> 00:08:25.237
but "The Mask"
was your first acting credit.

00:08:25.237 --> 00:08:26.873
-"The Mask" was my first film,

00:08:26.873 --> 00:08:29.508
and I turned 21
on the set of that film.

00:08:29.508 --> 00:08:31.811
It was such
an amazing experience.

00:08:31.811 --> 00:08:33.345
It changed my life forever.

00:08:33.345 --> 00:08:37.616
What was I going to do
with my life at 21 years old?

00:08:37.616 --> 00:08:39.384
Like, I had no plans, really.

00:08:39.384 --> 00:08:42.421
I was just kind of, like,
hustling as a model, you know?

00:08:42.421 --> 00:08:45.157
Like, just, I don't know.
Who knew?

00:08:45.157 --> 00:08:46.592
-Yeah.
-You know?

00:08:46.592 --> 00:08:48.160
-And you fell in love right
there and said, "This is it."

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:50.429
I got the bug.
This is what I want to do.

00:08:50.429 --> 00:08:53.832
-You know, it was just like,
at 21 years old, you're like,

00:08:53.832 --> 00:08:57.036
"Oh, I get to be around
a bunch of creative people

00:08:57.036 --> 00:09:00.172
who all work as this
amazing team for the same --

00:09:00.172 --> 00:09:02.475
you know, wanting to create
this one thing

00:09:02.475 --> 00:09:05.878
that everybody's
just completely committed to."

00:09:05.878 --> 00:09:10.783
And it was just this family
and comradery and just laughter

00:09:10.783 --> 00:09:12.485
all day long,
that I was just like,

00:09:12.485 --> 00:09:14.286
"I want to do this
all the time."

00:09:14.286 --> 00:09:17.022
-Was it Jim Carrey's
first film, as well?

00:09:17.022 --> 00:09:19.225
-No, but he had
already done "Ace Ventura,"

00:09:19.225 --> 00:09:20.692
but it hadn't come out yet.

00:09:20.692 --> 00:09:23.095
So, he was still the white dude
from "In Living Color."

00:09:23.095 --> 00:09:25.664
-[ Laughing ]

00:09:25.664 --> 00:09:27.165
Yeah, that's right.
Yeah.

00:09:27.165 --> 00:09:29.368
He was just
on our show last week.

00:09:29.368 --> 00:09:32.004
He has this new memoir out
that's really awesome

00:09:32.004 --> 00:09:34.239
and different
and trippy and cool.

00:09:34.239 --> 00:09:36.308
Was he great on set?

00:09:36.308 --> 00:09:38.277
-Oh, my God, Jim was the best.

00:09:38.277 --> 00:09:41.447
We had the best time,
really, honestly.

00:09:41.447 --> 00:09:45.317
It was kind of like the end of
the innocence, in a weird way.

00:09:45.317 --> 00:09:47.786
Because he hadn't
become Jim Carrey.

00:09:47.786 --> 00:09:49.421
You know?
So there, wasn't all that

00:09:49.421 --> 00:09:52.491
with him at that time,
even on set.

00:09:52.491 --> 00:09:54.393
You know,
we were just kind of free.

00:09:54.393 --> 00:09:57.462
And nobody knew
who I was, you know?

00:09:57.462 --> 00:09:59.898
-Isn't that great?
You don't forget that.

00:09:59.898 --> 00:10:02.201
-Yeah, it was really cool.
It was really cool.

00:10:02.201 --> 00:10:04.737
And Jim was always --
you know, he's such a jokester,

00:10:04.737 --> 00:10:08.074
and he's always
pulling jokes on everybody

00:10:08.074 --> 00:10:12.678
and making things
so surprising all the time.

00:10:12.678 --> 00:10:15.046
-[ Laughing ]
He's the best.

00:10:15.046 --> 00:10:19.351
I want to talk to you
about Avaline, your new wine!

00:10:19.351 --> 00:10:21.620
What are you talking about?!
What?!

00:10:21.620 --> 00:10:24.156
It's real, it's real!
It's here!

00:10:24.156 --> 00:10:25.824
It's gorgeous!
It's so fun!

00:10:25.824 --> 00:10:27.158
Let's talk about it.

00:10:27.158 --> 00:10:29.428
More with Cameron Diaz
when we come back, everybody.

